---
import evaluation from '../ml/evaluation.json';
import model from '../ml/model.json';
import TechTerm from './TechTerm.astro';

const { training } = model;
const sessionsPerCase = evaluation[0].sessions;

function outcome(alerts: number, sessions: number, medianDelay: number | null, alertExpected: boolean) {
  if (!alertExpected) {
    return alerts === 0 ? `No false alerts in ${sessions}` : `${alerts} false alerts in ${sessions}`;
  }
  const delay = medianDelay === null ? '' : `, median ${medianDelay} s into recovery`;
  return `Caught ${alerts} of ${sessions}${delay}`;
}
---
<section class="section" id="model">
  <p class="eyebrow">06 / Intelligence</p>
  <h2 class="section-title">Machine learning that runs where the astronaut is.</h2>
  <p class="section-lede">
    The model learns one astronaut's own response to an activity and reads every new window
    against it. Three questions, asked in order.
  </p>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <h3>Can this reading be trusted?</h3>
      <p>
        A <TechTerm id="signal-quality">signal quality check</TechTerm> looks for lost contact,
        missing data and motion first. An unreliable window is never scored; the astronaut is
        asked to adjust the band and measure again.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>How does it compare with this person?</h3>
      <p>
        A <TechTerm id="reference-model">neural network</TechTerm> predicts this astronaut's heart rate and skin temperature for the
        current moment of the activity. It is told the schedule, never the expected values:
        it learns those, and how much they normally vary, from reference sessions. The gap
        between a window and the prediction is its <TechTerm id="deviation-score">deviation score</TechTerm>.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <h3>Does the change persist?</h3>
      <p>
        A single odd reading is not an alert. Spectra raises one only when a deviation
        shows <TechTerm id="persistence">persistence</TechTerm> across consecutive windows.
        A <TechTerm id="cusum">trend sum</TechTerm> holds a warning while small deviations add up, and
        fixed rules name the <TechTerm id="likely-cause">likely cause</TechTerm>: movement, sensor, cabin or body.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    The model in the demo has two hidden layers and {training.parameters} parameters. It was
    trained beforehand on {training.sessions} synthetic sessions of normal recovery and runs
    in your browser for inference only. Its inputs are heart rate and skin temperature; the
    multispectral channels are not used yet.
  </p>

  <h3 class="subsection-title">Does the model add anything?</h3>
  <p>
    We ran the model and a plain <TechTerm id="statistical-baseline">statistical baseline</TechTerm> on {sessionsPerCase} sessions per
    case. None of these sessions was used to train or tune the model. Both use the same
    rule: three consecutive windows above the threshold.
  </p>
  <table class="eval-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Case</th>
        <th scope="col">Statistical baseline</th>
        <th scope="col">On-board model</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {evaluation.map((row) => (
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">{row.label}</th>
          <td data-column="Statistical baseline">
            {outcome(row.baseline.alerts, row.sessions, row.baseline.medianDelay, row.alertExpected)}
          </td>
          <td data-column="On-board model">
            {outcome(row.model.alerts, row.sessions, row.model.medianDelay, row.alertExpected)}
          </td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="source">
    Read this as a technical result. Every session is synthetic, and the simulator ties
    skin temperature to effort, which the model benefits from. Zero false alerts
    in {sessionsPerCase} sessions does not mean the rate is zero. None of this is clinical
    performance; it shows the pipeline works and what to measure next.
  </p>
</section>
